dian-home-price-us","gmpChartMode":"default","negRiskAugmented":false,"estimateValue":false,"cumulativeMarkets":false,"pendingDeployment":false,"deploying":false,"deployingTimestamp":"2026-07-01T19:54:31.135848Z","requiresTranslation":false,"eventMetadata":{"context_description":"**Persistent affordability constraints and subdued price appreciation are anchoring trader sentiment toward the <$419K bucket at 65.6% implied probability.** \n\nZillow’s national Home Value Index stood at approximately $372K through July 2026, reflecting just 1% year-over-year growth amid elevated 30-year mortgage rates near 6.5–7% and still-limited housing supply. NAR median existing-home sale prices hovered in the $397K–$411K range earlier in 2026, while Case-Shiller national readings showed similarly modest nominal gains of roughly 1% annualized through mid-year. Forecasts from NAR, Fannie Mae, and Zillow consistently point to 1–2% full-year appreciation for 2026, driven by wage growth outpacing price increases and buyer caution rather than rapid appreciation.\n\nWith only about six weeks remaining until the September 30 resolution date, the narrow window leaves little room for meaningful upward movement. The $419K–$426K range at 30.5% captures the modest upside scenario if late-summer data show continued low-single-digit gains, while higher buckets remain thinly priced given the slow trajectory and seasonal factors. Market-implied odds therefore reflect traders’ assessment that current fundamentals—high borrowing costs, constrained inventory, and steady but unspectacular demand—will keep median values near recent levels through quarter-end.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-19T17:18:03.247Z"},"version":"v1"}]
